00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:03.519 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  swapping frontline politics in  Washington for a real conflict zone  Donald Trump and Melania visited  American forces in Iraq posing for  photographs at a base to the west of  Baghdad the president even signing make  America great again caps some troops  brought to the event not normal for a  commander-in-chief with his soldiers who  aren't supposed to be political speaking  in a hanger Trump defended a surprise  decision to pull his military out of  neighboring Syria there will be a strong  deliberate and orderly withdrawal of US  forces from Syria very deliberate very  orderly  well maintaining the US presence in Iraq  to prevent an Isis resurgence and to  protect us interests and also to always  watch very closely over any potential  reformation of Isis and also to watch  over Iran the president said countries  in the region should step up to resolve  the conflict in Syria he also said the  United States wouldn't be a global  policeman a comment that will likely  revive concern within NATO over us  commitment to the Alliance America  shouldn't be doing the fighting for  every nation on earth  not being reimbursed in many cases at  all if they want us to do the fighting  they also have to pay a price and  sometimes that's also a monetary price  so we're not the suckers of the world  we're no longer the suckers folks the  president's first trip to a conflict  zone was under the cover of darkness and  with tight security for him and his team  but it wasn't all friendly smiles and  warm handshakes a plan for Trump to meet  the Naraku prime minister was canceled  apparently because of security reasons  and logistics the two men instead spoke  by phone President Trump will be hoping  for positive headlines following his  first visit to a conflict zone but when  he arrives back here in DC he will still  face questions over his decision to  withdraw  all US troops out of Syria especially if  there is any sign of Islamic state  trying to regroup in that country Trump  lost his defense secretary James mattis  last week in protest at the Syria  withdrawal security at home is also on  Trump's mind after a row with Democrats  over funding for a wall along the US  border with Mexico triggered a partial  government shutdown now in its fifth day  if Trump's feeling the pressure he's not  letting it show stopping off in Germany  on his way back from Iraq to visit us  cheap space there more selfies and grins  on display but given trump's desire for  European NATO allies to do more to  defend themselves some may wonder  whether one day he'll seek to bring US  troops in Europe home as well Deborah  Haynes Sky News Washington

